\details{
This is an engine block, not a front door: model packages call it
programmatically, so its tuning knobs (\code{max_iter}, \code{tol}, \code{damping})
sit in the signature rather than in a \code{control} list.
}
\section{Soft latent labels go in \code{weights}, not in \code{y}}{

The M-step maximizes the expected complete-data log-likelihood. For a
Bernoulli latent \code{z_i} carrying E-step posterior weight \code{w_i} that is
\deqn{Q = \sum_i [\, w_i \log p_i + (1 - w_i) \log (1 - p_i) \,],}
a \strong{weighted Bernoulli} log-likelihood, which carries no binomial
coefficient. Encode it as two rows per unit -- \code{y = 1} at weight \code{w_i} and
\code{y = 0} at weight \code{1 - w_i}:
\preformatted{list(y       = rep(c(1, 0), each = n),
     X       = rbind(X, X),
     weights = c(w, 1 - w),
     family  = "binomial")}
A fractional \code{y} on a binomial block is refused, and is not the same
objective: it asks for the exact binomial density at a non-integer
response, whose normalizer \code{lchoose(n, y)} is not zero there and depends on
\code{w}. On the weighted encoding the block's \code{log_marginal} is the Laplace
marginal of \code{Q}, an EM objective that increases across iterations.
}

\section{Standard errors}{

Each entry of \code{fits} carries \code{beta} (the fixed effects) and \code{se}, read off
that block's M-step curvature \code{H_beta}. That curvature is the
complete-data information at the converged weights: it treats the latent
labels as known, so these \code{se} understate the uncertainty by the
information the latent variable withholds.

The observed-data information follows from Louis' (1982) identity,
\deqn{I_{obs}(\theta) = E[-\nabla^2 \ell_c(\theta; z) \mid y] -
  \mathrm{Var}[\nabla \ell_c(\theta; z) \mid y],}
evaluated at the EM estimate. The first term is the blocks' \code{H_beta} (the
M-step objective is the expected complete-data log-likelihood when the
weights enter linearly, as in the weighted encoding below); the second,
the missing information, is estimated from \code{n_louis} hard draws
\code{z ~ draw_z(weights)}, each encoded by \code{m_step_encode()} and scored at
the estimate. The score covariance couples the blocks that share the
latent variable, so \code{vcov} is one joint matrix across blocks. The result
carries \code{vcov} (named \code{"<block>:<coef>"}), \code{se} (a named list per block)
and \code{louis} (\code{n_draws}, and \code{missing_fraction}, the share of each
coefficient's variance owed to the latent variable,
\code{1 - se_complete^2 / se^2}). It needs blocks without latent structure
(\code{re_list}, \code{spatial}, \code{prior}), whose fixed effects are the whole
parameter; otherwise, or when the estimated observed information is not
positive definite, \code{vcov} and \code{se} are \code{NULL} and \code{louis$unavailable}
says why. Dispersions set on a block (\code{phi}) are held at their values.
The draws consume the random-number stream.

\code{correction = "mi"} targets the same quantity by refitting each draw and
pooling with Rubin's rules. Its draws are taken at the fixed estimate, an
improper imputation that leaves out the estimate's own uncertainty, so its
pooled SE can run below the observed-information one.
}

\section{Posterior sampling}{

\code{correction = "gibbs"} alternates an exact draw from \code{p(Z | theta, y)}
with independence Metropolis-Hastings for \code{p(theta | Z, y)}. Here \code{theta}
contains every block's fixed effects and random-effect values. The
complete-data Laplace mode and full latent precision define a multivariate-t
proposal. The acceptance ratio uses the true complete-data density,
including priors, divided by the proposal density at both parameter states.
The current density is evaluated again after each latent update. The
resulting chain has invariant joint posterior \code{p(theta, Z | y)} under the
usual support and ergodicity conditions (Tanner and Wong, 1987; Robert and
Casella, 1999). Finite chains require convergence and Monte Carlo diagnostics.

The engine density evaluates the native family likelihood, retaining
normalizing constants, and normalized Gaussian coefficient and random-effect
priors. It reads the hard complete-data blocks from \code{m_step_encode(z, ...)}.
These must encode every factor of \code{p(y, z | theta)}, including the latent
label model. Observation weights must be 0 or 1 on this path. Supply
\code{log_complete} when the model needs additional factors or a different joint
density. The callback replaces the entire density, including priors.

Complete-data blocks currently support fixed effects and any number of
Gaussian random-effect terms with fixed covariance, including correlated
slopes. Dispersion is fixed. Nested, spatial and custom-likelihood blocks
are refused because their integrated fits supply no proposal for the full
conditional parameter vector. Deterministic \code{m_step_extra} updates are
refused on the Gibbs path. The parameter layout, coefficient order and
random-effect group identities must remain fixed across latent draws.

During sampling the E-step receives per-block \code{mode} and \code{beta} containing
the current parameter draw. It must use those values to compute the exact
latent conditional of the same joint model as the density. A finite MCMC
approximation to that conditional does not meet this contract. A separate
unbiased-likelihood extended-state construction is one alternative when
exact latent draws are unavailable (Andrieu and Roberts, 2009).

Each sweep costs one E-step, one complete-data Laplace solve per block,
and \code{1 + correction_control$mh_steps} joint-density evaluations. The
proposal factorization covers the full parameter vector. MI costs one
refit per block per imputation and retains the Tier 2 approximation.
}

\section{Tier}{

\code{"none"} and \code{"mi"} are Tier 2. \code{"gibbs"} is Tier 1 for the declared
joint posterior, conditional on the fixed dispersion and covariance.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
# Zero-inflated Poisson via EM: the E-step scores the posterior probability
# that each zero is non-structural. Those are soft labels, so the occupancy
# arm is the weighted Bernoulli above -- two rows per unit -- and the
# abundance arm weights each count by the same w.
set.seed(1)
n <- 200
z <- rbinom(n, 1, 0.7)
y <- rpois(n, 4) * z
X <- cbind(1, rnorm(n))

e_step <- function(fits, ...) {
  if (!length(fits)) return(list(weights = pmax(as.numeric(y > 0), 0.5)))
  psi <- plogis(drop(X \%*\% fits$occ$mode))
  lam <- exp(drop(X \%*\% fits$abund$mode))
  w <- ifelse(y > 0, 1, psi * exp(-lam) / (psi * exp(-lam) + (1 - psi)))
  list(weights = w)
}
m_step_encode <- function(weights, ...) {
  list(
    occ   = list(y = rep(c(1, 0), each = n), X = rbind(X, X),
                 weights = c(weights, 1 - weights), family = "binomial"),
    abund = list(y = y, X = X, family = "poisson", weights = weights)
  )
}

res <- tulpa_em_laplace(e_step, m_step_encode, verbose = FALSE)
res$converged
plogis(res$fits$occ$mode[1])    # P(non-structural), truth 0.7
exp(res$fits$abund$mode[1])     # abundance mean, truth 4
}
}
